.TH PAWELI-SYNTAX 7 "August 2026" "paweli" "Miscellaneous Information"
.SH NAME
paweli-syntax \- paweli language syntax reference
.SH DESCRIPTION
The lexical building blocks of the paweli language: comments, literals,
identifiers, keywords, and operators. See
.BR paweli (1)
for running and building programs, and
.BR paweli-language (7)
for the rest of the language.
.SH COMMENTS
.nf
// a single-line comment, runs to the end of the line

/// a multi-line comment
    opens and closes with three slashes,
    everything between is ignored
///
.fi
.PP
There is no
.B /* */
form. Line comments use
.BR // ,
block comments open and close with
.BR /// .
.SH IDENTIFIERS
An identifier starts with a letter or underscore, then continues with
letters, digits, or underscores:
.BR total ,
.BR _count ,
.BR player2 .
Identifiers are case-sensitive.
.SH KEYWORDS
These words are reserved and cannot be used as identifiers:
.TP
.B fn
declares a function
.TP
.B int
integer type
.TP
.B float
floating-point type
.TP
.B string
string type
.TP
.B bool
boolean type
.TP
.B if / else
conditional branching
.TP
.B while
condition-checked loop
.TP
.B for
C-style counted loop
.TP
.B break
exits the innermost loop immediately
.TP
.B continue
skips to the next iteration of the innermost loop
.TP
.B return
returns from a function
.TP
.B true / false
boolean literals
.TP
.B null
the null value
.TP
.B export
marks a function as part of a module's public interface
.TP
.B struct
declares a struct type
.SH LITERALS
.TP
.B Integer
.BR 0 ,
.BR 42 ,
.BR 1000 .
Decimal only; no
.BR 0x ,
.BR 0o ,
or
.B 0b
forms.
.TP
.B Float
.BR 0.0 ,
.BR 16.5 .
Must have digits on both sides of the
.B . .
.TP
.B String
.BR "hello" ,
.BR "line1\\enline2" .
Double-quoted; see escapes below.
.TP
.B Boolean
.BR true ,
.BR false .
.TP
.B Null
.BR null .
.PP
There is no negative-number literal syntax.
.B -7
is the unary
.B -
operator applied to
.BR 7 .
.PP
String escapes are
.BR "\\en" ,
.BR "\\et" ,
.BR "\\er" ,
.BR "\\e\\e" ,
.BR "\\e"" ,
and
.BR "\\e0"
(newline, tab, carriage return, backslash, double quote, null). Any other
character after a backslash is a compile error.
.SH OPERATORS
.TP
.B Arithmetic
.BR + ,
.BR - ,
.BR * ,
.BR / ,
.B %
.TP
.B Bitwise
.BR & ,
.BR | ,
.BR ^ ,
.BR ~ ,
.BR << ,
.BR >>
.TP
.B Comparison
.BR == ,
.BR != ,
.BR < ,
.BR <= ,
.BR > ,
.BR >=
.TP
.B Logical
.BR && ,
.BR || ,
.BR !
.TP
.B Assignment
.BR = ,
.BR += ,
.BR -= ,
.BR *= ,
.BR /= ,
.BR %= ,
.BR &= ,
.BR |= ,
.BR ^= ,
.BR <<= ,
.BR >>=
.TP
.B Member access
.B .
.TP
.B Indexing
.BR [ ] .
.TP
.B Function return type
.BR -> .
.PP
Precedence, lowest to highest: assignment,
.BR || ,
.BR && ,
.BR | ,
.BR ^ ,
.BR & ,
equality
.RB ( == ,
.BR != ),
comparison
.RB ( < ,
.BR <= ,
.BR > ,
.BR >= ),
shift
.RB ( << ,
.BR >> ),
addition
.RB ( + ,
.BR - ),
multiplication
.RB ( * ,
.BR / ,
.BR % ),
unary
.RB ( ! ,
.BR - ,
.BR ~ ),
then call, member, and index access
.RB ( () ,
.BR . ,
.BR [ ] ).
.PP
.B &&
and
.B ||
short-circuit: the right-hand side is not evaluated if the left side
already determines the result.
.PP
Bitwise
.BR & ,
.BR | ,
.BR ^ ,
.BR ~ ,
.BR << ,
and
.B >>
operate on the two's-complement bits of an
.B int
(never
.BR float ),
and sit at their own precedence tier between the logical operators and
equality, following C's placement:
.B "a & b == c"
parses as
.BR "a & (b == c)" ,
not
.BR "(a & b) == c" .
Parenthesize explicitly if that is not what is meant. A shift amount
outside 0..63 is a runtime error rather than the undefined behavior a
raw C shift would produce for the same input; a right shift of a
negative left-hand operand is arithmetic (sign-preserving).
.PP
A compound assignment,
.BR "target op= value" ,
is exactly equivalent to
.BR "target = target op value"
semantically, but not by literally expanding to that: for an index or
member target
.RB ( "arr[i] += 1" ,
.BR "s.f += 1" ),
the object and index/field sub-expressions are evaluated only once.
.BR "arr[f()] += 1"
calls
.B f()
exactly once. Like plain
.BR = ,
a compound assignment is itself an expression, producing the value that
was stored
.RB ( "y = (x += 1)"
sets
.I y
to
.I x
's new value). The right-hand side's type follows the same rules the
matching binary operator would
.RB ( +=
etc. promote int/float the way
.B +
does;
.B &=
etc. require both sides
.B int
the way
.B &
does), and the computed result has to still fit the target's own declared
type, the same check plain assignment makes.
.SH PUNCTUATION
.BR () ,
.BR {} ,
.BR , ,
and
.B ;
are grouping, blocks, argument/parameter separators, and statement
terminators.
.PP
.B [ ]
is array literals
.RB ( "[1, 2, 3]" )
and indexing
.RB ( "arr[i]" ).
.PP
.B :
and
.B ..
appear in only one place, the two shorter
.B for
forms:
.B "for (T name: a..b)"
(a range) and
.B "for (T name: expr)"
(an array or string). Neither is a general-purpose operator;
.B ..
in particular has no meaning outside that one syntactic position.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR paweli (1),
.BR paweli-language (7)
